From 740afb2c4d277d2bc3f9dff70c48c97a3f572adf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Facilis <dev@facilis.local>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 18:53:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(preview): route each preview request to the kernel that
 owns it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

handlePreview picked ONE window that hosts a kernel and posted every
/preview/<port>/ request there. That holds up while one Vivari kernel exists per
origin, and stops holding the moment two Studio tabs are open: both announce
themselves through vv-kernel-host, both run their dev server on the same in-VM
port (5173 in the common case), and the tab to boot second wins the lookup.
Measured: a fetch of /preview/5173/ issued BY tab A returned tab B's app, and
reloading tab A's preview painted tab B's project.

Route by the requesting client instead. A navigation belongs to the window that
started it (event.clientId — the Studio tab) and names the frame it is about to
create (resultingClientId); remember that pairing, so every later subresource of
the frame — which names only the frame — goes back to the same kernel. The
single-kernel heuristic stays as the fallback for a client we cannot attribute
(a revived SW has lost the map, and re-learns it on the next navigation).

The rest of the SW's per-origin config has the same shape of problem, so it
becomes per kernel too: a keep-prefix port announced by one tab is no longer
applied to the port of the same number in another (the persisted set is the
fallback for a revived SW), and an inbound ws/SSE frame is delivered to that
kernel's own preview windows instead of every top-level preview on the origin.

Verified with two real tabs (React in one, Vue in the other): both previews,
both forced reloads and every fetch stay on their own kernel.
